Stop the forecast reporting "could not read" as "found nothing" (P-284, P-285)

Step 1 of the P-280 work order: make the measurement honest, first because every
later measurement runs through this tool and because a false comment was still
there for the next person to build on.

THE COMMENT (P-285). SharedPermissionStorage.ContentKey said "The target never
enters it and the ordinal is normalised away, so two objects share a row exactly
when this agrees." The target does enter it. ObjectPermissions.ReadAt resolves
trustees against the object being read, and WellKnownTrustee renders S-1-5-10 -
PRINCIPAL SELF - as "This object (Alice)" on Alice and "This object (Bob)" on
Bob; ContentKey serialises the whole PermissionEntry, that resolved name
included. The code is right and P-279 is safe precisely because of this: Alice's
SELF entry and Bob's hash differently, so sharing can never show one object's
name against another object's permission. The comment was what a P-280 design
that would have done exactly that was built on, which is how it reached review.

It now says what is true, says that this is why P-279 is correct, and says the
price: a SELF entry never deduplicates, so every object carrying one gets its
own permission_entry row. S-1-5-10 sits in the default access control list of
essentially every user and computer, so the table P-279 built to shrink is
inflated by one row per such object, by an amount nobody has measured. The
entry_hash paragraph now points at ContentKey, because the paragraph headed "NOR
IS THE TARGET" is about the dropped column and reads at a glance as though the
target were nowhere near the row - which is the misreading that produced the
proposal. No test: a test that holds a comment to code is what this repository
deleted 260 of on 2026-09-11. The measurement and the SELF fix stay open as
P-285.

THE FORECAST (P-284). JudgementForecast counted every row in
object_security_descriptor as an object with readable permissions and then took
.DaclAces, discarding the parse state, completeness and errors that
SecurityDescriptorParser answers separately. A stored but unparseable descriptor
therefore arrived as one object with readable permissions and zero occurrences,
and a partly parsed one as an unqualified count of its survivors. The occurrence
total was never wrong - judging writes what parses - but the tool was reporting
could not compute as found nothing, which is the distinction the descriptor half
of the schema exists to keep.

Presence, unreadability and partial parsing are now three counts, each held at
the object and the descriptor level: one malformed descriptor inherited by fifty
thousand objects and fifty thousand malformed descriptors are the same object
count and very different problems. Readable is presence minus unreadable, by
subtraction rather than a second count, so the two cannot drift apart. Partly
parsed stays readable, because DaclState.Unreadable means a DACL declared
entries and yielded none, and striking such an object off would discard
permissions the product did read and must report. Errors are counted separately
again, because a descriptor can carry one and still have yielded every entry it
declared.

The declared count is still never substituted for the parsed one. The occurrence
total has to stay a prediction of the rows judging will write, and topping it up
with entries nobody could read would forecast rows that never exist while hiding
the shortfall inside the same number. The printed report gains a WHAT COULD NOT
BE READ block that is silent when all three counts are zero, so a page of
zeroes does not teach an operator to skip the place the warning appears. The
refusal at zero descriptors now says "stored" where it said "readable", and a
bundle whose descriptors all fail to parse is forecast at zero rather than
refused - refusing it would be the same substitution one layer up.

VERIFIED against next/artifacts/p279-after: 17,164,866 occurrences exactly,
unchanged, and still equal to that judgement's own row count. Every file in the
bundle byte-identical afterwards. Nine checks, each killed by at least one of
seven mutations with an explicit rebuild before running the mutated code and
again after restoring, every restore confirmed green. The mutations were:
readable = presence; the unreadable count removed; the partial count removed;
the declared ACE count read out of the DACL header and substituted for the
parsed one; unreadability attributed per descriptor rather than per object;
unreadable objects dropped from presence; and partly parsed collapsed into
unreadable. The fourth killed three checks including two occurrence totals,
which is the rule stated as a number.

AND THE BENCH COULD NOT HAVE FOUND THIS. p279-after holds zero unreadable, zero
partly parsed and zero object-type GUIDs, so all five new counts read zero
against it and every fixture had to be crafted by hand. That is F-S44-2 in
miniature and the argument for step 2 of the work order: a synthetic generated
by this codebase can only contain the assumptions the code already held.

